{
  "concept": "A continuous signal is reconstructed from its samples by convolving the impulse train with a sinc kernel: every sample drops a scaled, shifted copy of the sinc, and the superposition of all copies is the reconstructed waveform.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Three horizontal panels arranged left-to-right on a shared visual baseline",
    "Convolution operator symbol '∗' (drawn as a small circle/asterisk) between panel 1 and panel 2",
    "Equals sign '=' between panel 2 and panel 3",
    "Panel 1: horizontal black time axis with arrowhead, italic label 't' at right",
    "Panel 1: blue vertical impulse arrows at uniform spacing, positive arrows above axis and negative arrows below axis, heights tracing a smooth underlying waveform",
    "Panel 1: tick labels -T_s, 0, T_s, 2T_s under the sample positions",
    "Panel 2: horizontal black time axis with arrowhead and label 't', vertical axis arrow at center",
    "Panel 2: thick bright-green sinc-like kernel curve, peak value 1 at t=0, decaying oscillating side lobes, label '1' next to the peak on the vertical axis",
    "Panel 3: horizontal black time axis with arrowhead and label 't'",
    "Panel 3: the same blue sample impulse arrows, repeated",
    "Panel 3: one dashed green scaled/shifted sinc copy per sample (amplitude = sample value, centered at sample time)",
    "Panel 3: solid thick red reconstructed curve = sum of all green copies, passing exactly through every blue arrow tip",
    "Optional annotation in panel 3 marking zero-crossings of each sinc copy at all other sample instants",
    "Legend: blue = samples, dashed green = individual sinc contributions, solid red = reconstructed signal"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "samplingInterval",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "How sample spacing relative to the signal's bandwidth controls reconstruction: dense samples give narrow, well-separated sincs and an exact red curve, while coarse samples (past Nyquist) give wide overlapping sincs whose sum no longer matches the original signal — aliasing.",
      "label": "Sampling interval T_s",
      "range": [0.2, 1.6, 0.05],
      "default": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "copyVisibility",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the red reconstruction is nothing but the pointwise sum of the dashed green sinc copies — hiding the copies leaves a smooth curve, showing them exposes the superposition that built it.",
      "label": "Sinc copies",
      "options": ["Copies + sum", "Copies only", "Sum only"],
      "default": "Copies + sum"
    },
    {
      "id": "convolutionSweep",
      "type": "animation",
      "teaches": "That convolution is a sequential accumulation: the kernel is placed at each sample in turn, scaled by that sample, and added to a running total that grows into the full red envelope from left to right.",
      "label": "Sweep kernel across samples (build the sum)",
      "loop": false
    },
    {
      "id": "selectSample",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That each sample owns exactly one sinc copy, and that copy is zero at every other sample instant — which is why the red curve passes precisely through every sample tip.",
      "teaches_note": "click a blue arrow to isolate its green copy"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The three ingredients",
      "narration": "On the left, the signal exists only as blue sample impulses; in the middle sits the sinc reconstruction kernel with peak 1. The right panel is their convolution — the continuous signal recovered from discrete data.",
      "state": { "samplingInterval": 0.5, "copyVisibility": "Copies + sum" }
    },
    {
      "title": "One sample, one sinc",
      "narration": "Hide the sum and look only at the dashed green curves: each blue sample plants a sinc scaled to its own height and centered at its own time. Every copy crosses zero at all the other sample instants.",
      "state": { "samplingInterval": 0.5, "copyVisibility": "Copies only" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweeping the convolution",
      "narration": "Step the kernel across the samples one at a time and watch the running total accumulate from left to right — convolution is literally this shift-scale-add process.",
      "state": { "samplingInterval": 0.5, "copyVisibility": "Copies + sum" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The sum is the signal",
      "narration": "Hide the copies and only the red curve remains: a smooth continuous waveform that passes exactly through every blue sample tip. Nothing was invented — the samples fully determined it.",
      "state": { "samplingInterval": 0.5, "copyVisibility": "Sum only" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sampling too slowly",
      "narration": "Widen T_s past the Nyquist limit: the sinc copies become broad and overlap heavily, and the red reconstruction now drifts away from the original waveform between samples — this is aliasing.",
      "state": { "samplingInterval": 1.4, "copyVisibility": "Copies + sum" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sampling densely",
      "narration": "Shrink T_s: narrow, tightly packed sincs reconstruct the signal almost perfectly. Reconstruction quality is set by sample spacing, not by the kernel's shape.",
      "state": { "samplingInterval": 0.25, "copyVisibility": "Sum only" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Reconstruction is not guessing between samples — each sample simply contributes its own scaled sinc that is zero at every other sample, so the sum automatically threads through all samples and is the unique bandlimited signal behind them.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide, short, three-panel layout on a white background with a shared horizontal baseline; panels separated by a large '∗' (convolution) symbol and a large '=' sign, both in black. Axes are thick black lines with solid triangular arrowheads and an italic serif 't' at the right end. Panel 1 (left third): blue impulse arrows with triangular heads at uniform spacing, several pointing up with varying heights and two or three pointing down below the axis, heights following a smooth low-frequency waveform; serif italic tick labels -T_s, 0, T_s, 2T_s below the axis near the center. Panel 2 (middle third): a short vertical axis arrow at center, a thick bright-green (lime) sinc curve peaking at 1 on that axis with symmetric decaying side lobes crossing zero at regular intervals; small label '1' just left of the peak. Panel 3 (right third): the same blue impulses redrawn, overlaid by thin dashed lime-green scaled sinc copies (one per sample, negative-amplitude copies flipped below the axis), and a thick solid red curve on top that is their pointwise sum and visibly touches each blue arrow tip. During the sweep animation, the currently active kernel copy should be drawn bolder and the red curve should be drawn only up to the swept position. Keep stroke weights heavy and the palette limited to black, blue, lime green, and red, matching a textbook line-art style."
}

